  "incident": [
   {
    "category": "infrastructure",
    "close_code": "Solved (Permanently)",
    "close_notes": "Checkout catalog lookups unavailable - RESOLVED. One storefront tier could not reach its backing datastores; the on-call first suspected the release that had shipped that day and were about to roll it back. Before rolling back we traced the delivery path end to end and the release was healthy, so the deploy was NOT the cause. A maintenance window that night had re-established a cross-VPC connection and re-applied tiered firewalling; comparing the affected tier's firewalling, subnet access-lists and routing against its healthy peer tiers - IN BOTH DIRECTIONS and on BOTH ENDS of the connection - showed the outbound allowances, the return routing and the far-end admittances had all been dropped for that one tier while the peers were intact. We restored each hop to the peer baseline (specific networks and ports, never a blanket 0.0.0.0/0), cleaned up the stale records the retired network left behind, and service recovered. Lesson: verify the reported cause; a connection needs BOTH directions and BOTH ends; restore to the peer baseline with least privilege; leave no dangling records. The release was left untouched. Closed.",
    "closed_at": "2026-05-09 15:30:00",
    "description": "A storefront tier could not reach its backing datastores; a same-day release was suspected. See close notes.",
    "number": "INC0005990",
    "priority": "2",
    "short_description": "Storefront tier could not reach datastores - blamed on a release, was a maintenance-window change",
    "state": "7",
